        "title": "The burning ghats provide an interesting perspective on life and death",
        "text": "I felt it was really important to visit the burning ghats because public cremation is such an important ritual in Hindu life, but I also felt a little sheepish about potential voyeurism. It was definitely a solemn visit, but I was surprised to discover that next to us (on the opposite bank from the families and ceremonies) many Nepali people were gathered to watch the proceedings, and that they do not know the deceased either. We were told that people come to educate themselves so that they will be familiar with the process when they need to perform it for their loved ones. As an example, the young woman in front of me had her 4-year-old on her lap watching as an ambulance delivered a shrouded corpse to the waiting relatives. In general, once the deceased arrive, the families pray at one of the riverside shrines and then dip the deceased's feet into the river. From there they move down river to the part of the bank where the cremations are performed. We saw a religious man with an earbud and smartphone chanting into a mic. We wondered if he was performing prayers for a family member who could not attend in person.",
